Something 's not right. Still too complex.  Ah. Lists are recursive.  Their treatment should be recursive as well.  Let's see…<p><pre><code>  void remove_if(node ** head, remove_fn rm)
  {
      node * entry = *head;
      if (rm(entry))
      {
          *head = entry->next;
          free(entry);
      }
      else
          head = &entry->next;
      remove_if(head, rm);
   }
</code></pre>
Ahh, much better:  Now we can refactor:<p><pre><code>  void remove_head(node ** head)
  {
      node * entry = *head;
      *head = entry->next;
      free(entry);
  }

  void remove_if(node ** head, remove_fn rm)
  {
      if (rm(*head))
          remove_head(head)
      else
          head = &entry->next;
      remove_if(head, rm);
   }
</code></pre>
I'm sure GCC will be able to optimize away the tail call.<p>Edit: oops, I forgot to test for empty lists, which was implicit in the for loop I set out to remove…
